{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To determine the largest possible integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 942! \\) is divisible by \\( 15^n \\), we need to find the highest power of \\( 15 \\) that divides \\( 942! \\). Since \\( 15 = 3 \\times 5 \\), we need to find the highest powers of \\( 3 \\) and \\( 5 \\) that divide \\( 942! \\), and then the limiting factor will be the power of \\( 5 \\) (since \\( 5 \\) is less frequent than \\( 3 \\) in the factorization of \\( 942! \\)).\n\nFirst, we use de Polignac's (Legendre's) formula to find the highest power of a prime \\( p \\) dividing \\( n! \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^k} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the highest power of \\( 5 \\) dividing \\( 942! \\)\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^4} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^6} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^7} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5^8} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{625} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{942}{3125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1287,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,8253,279,7772,3204,7546,17767,308,1124,8,1741,429,17767,220,24,19,17,0,1124,8,374,74916,553,17767,220,16,20,86167,1124,701,582,1184,311,1477,279,8426,2355,315,17767,220,16,20,1124,8,429,64828,17767,220,24,19,17,0,1124,568,8704,17767,220,16,20,284,220,18,1124,15136,220,20,1124,701,582,1184,311,1477,279,8426,13430,315,17767,220,18,1124,8,323,17767,220,20,1124,8,4